#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38bc38bc-4117-5713-bcc7-f41bb36fdc4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335856} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdc42b4-c773-5263-b22c-7ab06b99321c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335857} {:answer/id #uuid "8a68e4a6-0103-5b47-bebc-81ac24dd3af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335858} {:answer/id #uuid "6240f72a-0d02-5d66-bf9c-695aad894866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335859} {:answer/id #uuid "643a4bb6-ecd5-5d11-bb9b-2e1773973ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcdc42b4-c773-5263-b22c-7ab06b99321c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335857} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d234ec-66e7-50ac-b720-163a2cf9d3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "698299c1-e40e-528b-a38d-66f6a77ea0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89347" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/a2f0f570ecc68de1a5b34fb3bd1b341fc83a728b026b035dde848dbf986e441b.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/e7ef7fd90c828767359daf69f68f406d61e3f82ad86b1f6b3a9480c99dedadf8.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ we get the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].\n$$"}]}
